          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/confidence-scoring.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Confidence Scoring\\ndescription: A qualitative reliability signal attached to a wiki page.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Confidence-Scoring\\n  - concept/Knowledge-Base\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two sources; describes core mechanism without extensive validation.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nConfidence scoring assigns a qualitative reliability signal to a page.\\n\\n## Rationale\\n\\nThe score flags which pages deserve re-verification, not which are \\u0027correct\\u0027. It is assigned at creation and\\nrevisited when new sources touch the same topic.\\n\\nConfidence scoring is **not a truth score**. Instead, it reflects source reliability indicators: the number of\\nindependent sources backing the page, the authority of those sources, and the age of the source material\\nrelative to how fast the topic evolves. A high-confidence page may contain outdated information if its\\nsources have aged; a low-confidence page may be factually sound but simply under-sourced. The score tells\\nyou which pages need re-verification, not which conclusions to trust.\\n\u0022}"
        }
